across Mystic Avenue, it goes under Mystic Avenue and continues up and really serves as a drainage resource for the neighborhood up above here. 300 Mystic Avenue formerly was owned by the Mass Department of Transportation. It was a district four MassDOT office. There was a sign shop there and some maintenance functions. The state over a number of years of conversation

35
00:15:32,628 --> 00:15:59,037
that we had with them decided that they could surplus the property. And perhaps if they were to leave, it might allow some economic development on that site. So maybe three years ago or four years ago, the state did put the property up for sale on a sealed bid basis. And we bid on it. We ended up being the winning bidder in the sealed bid process.

36
00:15:59,999 --> 00:16:29,041
And then the state, the MassDOT has actually relocated. And you've probably seen they have built a new facility behind the VFW farther up Mystic Avenue toward Medbridge Square. So they have relocated. We have since leased that building to a building supply products company on a temporary basis. So there is some activity there. And then continuing south is 312 to 326 Mystic Avenue. There are a number of tenants in there.

37
00:16:29,598 --> 00:16:59,315
I will just say that in general, all of the tenants who are here of ours are there on an interim basis. We have been very clear with the occupants that is our hope to redevelop the property and ultimately take these buildings down. So all of them know that we do have a plan or an aspiration to redevelop the site. But just, you know, the site looking at the attributes locationally, it has great access to Route 93 and great visibility on Route 93.

38
00:17:00,108 --> 00:17:25,117
It has excellent visibility and access on Mystic Avenue and to Route 16. Eight acres this close to Boston with this kind of transportation access is not easy to find. There is MBTA bus service on Mystic Avenue. As you probably know, the 95 bus goes right by here. It goes up into the square today and goes down to Sullivan Square.

39
00:17:25,319 --> 00:17:53,416
There are two bus stops on our side of Mystic Avenue along our frontage, and there are two other bus stops on the other side of Mystic Avenue. So there's pretty good transportation access because of the T. And the site is very close to Tufts. It's about a mile from Tufts, Tufts University. And also because of the highway access and the T access, it has pretty good access to MIT and Harvard, as well as BU and Northeastern and Boston College.

40
00:17:54,378 --> 00:18:23,521
The site really, what we see there today, these single story buildings are reflective of the condition of the economy and the real estate market maybe 15, 20, 30 years ago. We think that there's a brighter future for this site that will have some benefits for the city if we are able to redevelop it. And that's what we would like to talk with you about this evening. And the concept we have for the site, and I put it here at the top, it says Medford Life Science Park.

41
00:18:23,909 --> 00:18:50,048
And what we have in mind is to take advantage of the current explosion in life science activity that's taking place in the Boston area. And I'm sure you're seeing it in the news just as we are. It's really perhaps the most active growth sector in the Massachusetts economy today. I would say that's a function of several fortuitous factors that are coming together

42
00:18:50,504 --> 00:19:16,812
to create that explosion. Certainly one of them is the base of medical and healthcare expertise that exists here in the Boston area. We know that people come from around the world for medical care here in Boston because it's absolutely tops. In addition to that, we have some of the world's leading universities here. As I mentioned, Tufts and BU, BC, Northeastern, MIT and Harvard among many.

43
00:19:17,707 --> 00:19:45,196
And those universities are producing graduates in disciplines that are necessary for life science at the bachelor's level, at the master's level, at the PhD level. There already is a critical mass of life science enterprises here in the Malden area that are active in genomics and pharmaceuticals, among others, medical devices. So that's a kind of core that exists in the Boston area.

44
00:19:45,955 --> 00:20:10,424
And then lastly, we're very fortunate to have a very active venture capital investment resource here in the Boston area. Boston ranks number one worldwide in investments of venture capital in life sciences, which is really pretty amazing. So taking all of those factors together, it's a very fertile area for life science growth and development.

45
00:20:10,694 --> 00:20:36,158
And those factors are really why we're seeing the explosion of life science activity in Massachusetts and certainly in the Boston area. Historically, Cambridge was known as, I would say, maybe the ground zero for life science. But because the growth has been so explosive, we're seeing the growth move into Boston. We're seeing it moved out to Watertown, Lexington. It's coming over to Charlestown.

46
00:20:36,867 --> 00:21:05,926
And we think that Medford can capture its share or more of this life science group. So coming back to our site, here we have eight acres in very close proximity to Boston with excellent transportation access, highway access, MBTA bus, and ultimately rapid transit and commuter rail access, proximity to the universities, Tufts, Harvard, MIT, BU, Northeastern, BC,

47
00:21:06,668 --> 00:21:35,204
And all those factors suggest that if a project can be developed here, a life science focused project with enough critical mass that it can put Medford on the map for the life science sector and begin to attract large numbers and significant life science activity. And I will mention there already is life science activity taking place in Medford. So I think that's a very good sign

48
00:21:35,525 --> 00:22:02,947
We know there are buildings where life science activity is taking place. What this project will do is create critical mass in one place. And what we have in mind, and we'll share with you the details, is 1.2 million square feet of life science space, research and development, and related office space in four buildings that would be eight stories that would be developed sequentially, potentially, or perhaps all at once across this site.

49
00:22:04,752 --> 00:22:31,280
So with this, I think, and the last thing I'll mention is the zoning. The current zoning really is the commercial two zoning really contemplates the kind of buildings that we see today on the site. It really is not reflective of the kind of facilities that life science companies are demanding today. And fortunately, the city within the past year

50
00:22:31,583 --> 00:23:00,406
has done a major revision of the zoning bylaw. A lot of work has gone into it by many, many people within the city. And there was a new zoning bylaw that has been put together very carefully. And in that zoning bylaw is a new provision called planned development district. And that allows the city on a case by case basis to consider modifying the zoning in specific places through an overlay process.

51
00:23:01,148 --> 00:23:25,752
that would create a separate and new zoning overlay district to be applied where the city feels that it might be beneficial. And that overlay would have specific parameters designed to facilitate development of a particular kind of project. Again, if the city feels it would be beneficial. What we have in mind is to see if we can work with the city, if the city would be receptive,

52
00:23:26,579 --> 00:23:56,178
to considering to put in place a planned commercial development district that would encompass the eight acres that we're showing here in blue. And that planned commercial development district would have zoning parameters that are designed to allow the life science project that we have in mind, the Medford Life Science Park. It would be a master plan park, again, designed to create the critical mass, but also provide a number of benefits to the community.

53
00:23:57,123 --> 00:24:23,650
So the process for the creation of a planned development district, if the city is receptive, is spelled out in the zoning bylaw. It requires the project proponent, which in this case is combined properties, to assemble quite a list of deliverables that would include traffic information, civil information, survey information, architectural and planning information,

54
00:24:24,207 --> 00:24:51,292
into a package that would be presented to the community development board. And we are in the early stages of assembling the material that the zoning bylaw requires. We probably, if I had to guess, maybe 60 days away from having that package together. Once we have the package together, we would then provide it to the community development board and they would then be able to hold a public hearing on it.

55
00:24:52,017 --> 00:25:21,970
So really the purpose for our meeting with you folks this evening and for the meeting that we had on Monday is to let you know about our idea for this project, to get some feedback from you, some questions from you that would help inform the package that we are putting together for the Community Development Board so that it reflects questions and concerns that you folks might have. Once the package is together, as I said, it will go to the Community Development Board. They will hold a public hearing.

56
00:25:22,780 --> 00:25:50,303
And if in fact the Community Development Board finds that the project, as we are proposing it, would in fact be beneficial to the city, they can then vote to send the project, including our suggested zoning parameters, to the City Council for the City Council's consideration. The City Council would take it up at that point as part of their ordinary procedure, their ordinary business.

57
00:25:51,080 --> 00:26:17,354
And if they find that they're in agreement with the Community Development Board, that in fact the project is beneficial, they could then vote to enact it and they would send it to the mayor's desk. So that's roughly how the local process would work as it is set out in the Medford Zoning Bylaw. That would not be the end of the permit process if in fact those steps do take place

58
00:26:17,978 --> 00:26:47,864
and the zoning overlay is applied to the project. We do need to then interact both with the Mass Department of Transportation, because Mystic Avenue is under their purview, in a sense is owned by them. And we are going to be proposing some changes to Mystic Avenue, which we think will be beneficial to the city, but we need MassDOT to agree to work with us collaboratively. And then the state has a environmental review process

59
00:26:48,252 --> 00:27:16,484
which is related to what MassDOT would be doing. And there is an application package that we have to put together for them, an environmental report, which will take some time for them to review and reach a final report on. So that could be, we don't know for sure, but could perhaps take 12 months to complete those steps after a point at which the city might decide to enact the zoning overlay. And just to clarify, the zoning overlay

60
00:27:17,058 --> 00:27:42,387
as it might sound, would apply on top of the existing zoning. So it doesn't change the underlying zoning. The underlying commercial 2 zoning would remain. The overlay, as the name would suggest, goes on top of it. And if it is enacted, would encompass these provisions, which would allow us to do this project. So that's a summary. And I think at this point, I will

61
00:27:43,248 --> 00:28:11,412
